From: edde746 <86283021+edde746@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 22:10:24 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] avformat/tls_openssl: read ahead

Without read-ahead OpenSSL reads each TLS record off the socket in two
reads through the TCP protocol, one for the 5-byte header and one for
the body, and each is a poll() and a recv(). Streaming a 38 Mbit/s file
over HTTPS on an Apple TV, those reads averaged 7.6 KB and mpv's demuxer
thread spent 2.7% of a core under SSL_read().

Turn read-ahead on with a 64 KiB buffer, so one read takes whatever
records have arrived. On the same stream the reads average about 25 KB
and the demuxer thread uses about a quarter less CPU. Only TLS changes;
DTLS reads whole datagrams already.

Data read ahead waits in OpenSSL instead of the socket. FFmpeg's own
reads go through SSL_read() and get it first, but a caller that polls
the socket before reading, as RTSP does for its control connection
while the media runs over UDP, can miss a message queued behind another
one until more data arrives. The unread rest of a record already waited
in OpenSSL the same way before.

diff --git a/libavformat/tls_openssl.c b/libavformat/tls_openssl.c
index 0075288d61..10837e45c4 100644
--- a/libavformat/tls_openssl.c
+++ b/libavformat/tls_openssl.c
@@ -847,6 +847,11 @@ static int tls_open(URLContext *h, const char *uri, int flags, AVDictionary **op
         goto fail;
     }
     SSL_set_ex_data(c->ssl, 0, c);
+    // Without read-ahead OpenSSL reads each record off the socket in two
+    // reads through the TCP protocol, the 5-byte header and then the body.
+    // With it, one read takes whatever records have arrived, up to 64 KiB.
+    SSL_set_read_ahead(c->ssl, 1);
+    SSL_set_default_read_buffer_len(c->ssl, 64 * 1024);
     SSL_CTX_set_info_callback(c->ctx, openssl_info_callback);
 
     if (s->is_dtls) {
